  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA broad ridging pattern will persist through the work week \nbefore troughing off the southern California coast begins to \nmove ashore by Thursday morning. Some differences remain among \nclusters regarding to the speed of the trough ejection into the \nplains with some as early Friday. Regardless, rain chances \nincrease Friday and linger into Saturday. The quick moving\nshortwave will have little moisture to work with and almost all\nensemble members keep rainfall amounts around a quarter inch or\nless. Isolated thunderstorms cannot be ruled out given the\nunusual warmth and instability. \n\nOther notable features through the period will be strong winds \nFriday and Saturday, initially out of the south and turning to \nthe west Saturday after a strong cold front passes through \nFriday night. The most likely range for top wind gusts are 35-45\nmph both days, though there is moderate confidence for isolated\ngusts around 50 mph. Temperatures will remain well above \nseasonal averages for the next few days, before a more typical \nnorthwest upper level flow returns brings high temperatures in \nthe 40s by Sunday/Monday.\n\nOverall, enjoy a few more days of dry weather and 60s to 70s \nfor highs this week as they will become increasingly fleeting in\nthe coming weeks with seasonal normals dropping into the 40s by\nthe end of the month for highs.",
